      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 67-year-old gentleman presented with a 1-day history of left foot pain. He had recently been discharged following a coronary artery bypass graft; during the admission, he had received an intravenous heparin infusion. Examination revealed black-purple discolouration of the first and second digits of the left foot with pitting oedema to the level of the knee. The posterior tibial and dorsalis pedis pulses were both shown to be patent. A new thrombocytopenia was noted. Ultrasound imaging revealed multiple deep vein thrombosis. The history of recent heparin exposure coupled with venous gangrene secondary to deep vein thrombosis was consistent with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. He was treated acutely with intravenous danaparoid and later with warfarin. There was complete resolution of the venous gangrene at 1 month follow-up."},
